<title>USA: U.S. Jan wholesale inventories rose 1.0 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. Jan wholesale inventories rose 1.0 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-03-11</dateline>
<text>
<p>Commerce Department report</p>
<p>of wholesale inventories and sales, seasonally adjusted.</p>
<p> (Percent Changes)     Jan    Dec--Rev from  Jan97/96</p>
<p> Inventories Total     1.0    0.1    -0.6	1.8</p>
<p> Sales Total	     1.0    UNCH    0.2	6.8</p>
<p> (Billions of dlrs)   Jan     Dec--Rev from   Jan96</p>
<p> Inventories Total  260.80  258.11  256.34   256.19</p>
<p> -Durable Goods     164.64  163.33  162.44   162.87</p>
<p> --Cars/Parts	  27.71   26.73   26.46    27.52</p>
<p> -Non-Durable Goods  96.16   94.77   93.90    93.32</p>
<p> --Petroleum Prods.   5.73    5.80    5.78     5.09</p>
<p> (Billions of dlrs)   Jan     Dec--Rev from   Jan96</p>
<p>  Sales Total	 205.79  203.84  204.34   192.68</p>
<p> -Durable Goods     104.76  104.24  104.42    99.71</p>
<p> --Cars/Parts	  17.51   17.64   17.44    17.28</p>
<p> -Non-Durable Goods 101.03   99.60   99.92    92.97</p>
<p> --Petroleum Prods.  17.51   17.22   17.14    13.84</p>
<p>The department said the 1.0 percent increase in January wholesale inventories was the largest for any month since April 1996 when they rose 1.2 percent. </p>
<p>The 1.0 percent sales increase in January was the biggest monthly gain since a 1.8 percent rise in July last year, the department said.</p>
<p>The January stock-to-sales ratio for merchant wholesalers, seasonally adjusted, was unchanged from December at 1.27 months' worth. Previously, the department said the December stock-to-sales ratio was 1.25 months.</p>
<p>The ratio is a measure of how many months it would take to sell existing inventories at the current sales pace.</p>
</text>
